,"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/1h7qzj","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/1h7qzj/outis_fight_ai_spam_by_generating_sending"},{"short_id":"crlwst","created_at":"2026-10-01T00:13:31.010-05:00","title":"Typeclasses vs Modules","url":"https://sm2n.ca/articles/typeclasses-vs-modules/","score":30,"flags":0,"comment_count":5,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"abhin4v","user_is_author":false,"tags":["haskell","ml","plt"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/crlwst","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/crlwst/typeclasses_vs_modules"},{"short_id":"zcr7in","created_at":"2026-10-01T07:16:51.385-05:00","title":"Is sandboxing sufficient to contain rogue agents?","url":"https://blog.cryptographyengineering.com/2026/09/30/is-sandboxing-sufficient-to-contain-rogue-agents/","score":12,"flags":0,"comment_count":6,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"carlana","user_is_author":false,"tags":["ai","security"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/zcr7in","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/zcr7in/is_sandboxing_sufficient_contain_rogue"},{"short_id":"lzlkbr","created_at":"2026-10-01T01:02:28.542-05:00","title":"Reviving Valve's 15-year-old e-book","url":"https://nikolan.net/posts/portal2/","score":33,"flags":0,"comment_count":1,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"videah","user_is_author":false,"tags":["games","reversing","vibecoding"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/lzlkbr","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/lzlkbr/reviving_valve_s_15_year_old_e_book"},{"short_id":"8ckgq8","created_at":"2026-10-01T06:43:16.865-05:00","title":"WSL containers are now generally available","url":"https://blogs.windows.com/windowsdeveloper/2026/09/29/wsl-containers-now-generally-available/","score":12,"flags":0,"comment_count":3,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"abareplace","user_is_author":false,"tags":["virtualization","windows"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/8ckgq8","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/8ckgq8/wsl_containers_are_now_generally"},{"short_id":"hbjy0b","created_at":"2026-09-30T21:03:34.978-05:00","title":"The AI Pascal’s Wager","url":"https://ploum.net/2026-10-01-pascal_wager.html","score":23,"flags":0,"comment_count":37,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"cgrinds","user_is_author":false,"tags":["vibecoding"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/hbjy0b","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/hbjy0b/ai_pascal_s_wager"},{"short_id":"ajtnc6","created_at":"2026-10-01T09:12:03.343-05:00","title":"The Second Golden Spike: Memory Safety Across the Valen/Rust Boundary","url":"https://verdagon.dev/blog/boundary-memory-safety","score":5,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"Verdagon","user_is_author":true,"tags":["compilers","performance","plt","rust"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/ajtnc6","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/ajtnc6/second_golden_spike_memory_safety_across"},{"short_id":"bag41j","created_at":"2026-09-30T10:55:21.340-05:00","title":"Hanami, Why?: Introductions","url":"https://aaronmallen.me/writing/hanami-why-introductions","score":48,"flags":1,"comment_count":2,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"aaronmallen","user_is_author":true,"tags":["ruby"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/bag41j","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/bag41j/hanami_why_introductions"},{"short_id":"sqyhgt","created_at":"2026-09-30T19:02:09.641-05:00","title":"Major rsync upgrade in Debian because of 33 CVEs","url":"","score":23,"flags":3,"comment_count":5,"description":"\u003cstrong\u003eapt-listchanges --which=both -f text --since=3.4.1+ds1-5+deb13u4 \u003cbr\u003e\n/var/cache/apt/archives/rsync_3.5.0+ds1-0+deb13u1_amd64.deb\napt-listchanges: Reading changelogs...\napt-listchanges: News\u003c/strong\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ersync (3.5.0+ds1-0+deb13u1) trixie-security; urgency=medium\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn order to fix 33 CVEs, I have decided to bump the package to 3.5.0 rather\nthan backporting all patches individually. After analysing the extra changes\nfrom the bump, not included in the CVE fixes, I have concluded this approach\ncarries the lower amount of risk compared to the alternative.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis update contains behavior changes, all of which stems from the CVE fixes\nthemselves, not exclusive to the version bump. The ones most likely to break\nan existing setup are listed here; /usr/share/doc/rsync/NEWS.md.gz has the\nfull list.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOperator-supplied paths are no longer followed through untrusted symlinks.\nThe destination directory and the arguments to --backup-dir, --temp-dir,\n--partial-dir, --link-dest, --compare-dest, --copy-dest, --log-file,\n--password-file, --files-from, --include-from, --exclude-from,\n--write-batch, --read-batch and --filter merge files are now resolved one\ncomponent at a time, following a symlink only when it is owned by root or\nby the user running rsync; one owned by anyone else is refused with\n\"refusing to follow a symlink owned by an untrusted user\".\n--insecure-links restores the old behaviour, but it is local only and a\ndaemon never honours it. For a single trusted module, set\n\"insecure links = yes\" in that module instead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003errsync now refuses --debug on every invocation. When restricted to a\nsubdirectory it additionally denies --copy-unsafe-links, passes the new\n--confine-root so the server will not resolve a client-named filter merge\nfile outside that directory, and passes --drop-D when receiving, so an\nupload can no longer create devices or special files there (\"skipping\nnon-regular file\"). A plain \"rsync -a\" otherwise still works.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e--chmod=a+s now sets both the setuid and setgid bits, matching chmod(1);\nit previously set setuid alone.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ersyncd changes that can change who gets in:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\"proxy protocol = true\" without \"proxy protocol hosts\" now rejects\nevery connection and warns at startup, instead of trusting a\nclient-supplied PROXY header.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\"hosts deny\" now fails closed when a configured hostname cannot be\nresolved (with \"forward lookup\", the default), so a host previously\nadmitted by an unresolvable deny entry is now blocked.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\"auth users\" values that start with a comma now split on commas alone,\nas documented, so a deny or :ro rule naming a group whose name contains\na space now takes effect where it was silently ignored.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\"hosts allow\" / \"hosts deny\" patterns now fold case inside a [...]\nbracket expression as well, so a rule such as [A-Z]* matches hosts it\nused to miss.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eA client-requested --compress-threads is capped at 8.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ersync-ssl now verifies the server certificate. The default openssl\nbackend additionally binds it to the requested hostname, so a certificate\nvalid for some other name is now rejected. The stunnel and gnutls\nbackends refuse to run unless RSYNC_SSL_CA_CERT is set, or\nRSYNC_SSL_ALLOW_INSECURE_STUNNEL=1 / RSYNC_SSL_ALLOW_INSECURE_GNUTLS=1 is\nset to opt out.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e-- Samuel Henrique \u003ca href=\"mailto:samueloph@debian.org\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003esamueloph@debian.org\u003c/a\u003e  Tue, 15 Sep 2026 18:46:30 -0700\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"apt-listchanges --which=both -f text --since=3.4.1+ds1-5+deb13u4 \\\r\n  /var/cache/apt/archives/rsync_3.5.0+ds1-0+deb13u1_amd64.deb\r\napt-listchanges: Reading changelogs...\r\napt-listchanges: News\r\n---------------------\r\n\r\nrsync (3.5.0+ds1-0+deb13u1) trixie-security; urgency=medium\r\n\r\n  In order to fix 33 CVEs, I have decided to bump the package to 3.5.0 rather\r\n  than backporting all patches individually. After analysing the extra changes\r\n  from the bump, not included in the CVE fixes, I have concluded this approach\r\n  carries the lower amount of risk compared to the alternative.\r\n\r\n  This update contains behavior changes, all of which stems from the CVE fixes\r\n  themselves, not exclusive to the version bump. The ones most likely to break\r\n  an existing setup are listed here; /usr/share/doc/rsync/NEWS.md.gz has the\r\n  full list.\r\n\r\n  Operator-supplied paths are no longer followed through untrusted symlinks.\r\n  The destination directory and the arguments to --backup-dir, --temp-dir,\r\n  --partial-dir, --link-dest, --compare-dest, --copy-dest, --log-file,\r\n  --password-file, --files-from, --include-from, --exclude-from,\r\n  --write-batch, --read-batch and --filter merge files are now resolved one\r\n  component at a time, following a symlink only when it is owned by root or\r\n  by the user running rsync; one owned by anyone else is refused with\r\n  \"refusing to follow a symlink owned by an untrusted user\".\r\n  --insecure-links restores the old behaviour, but it is local only and a\r\n  daemon never honours it. For a single trusted module, set\r\n  \"insecure links = yes\" in that module instead.\r\n\r\n  rrsync now refuses --debug on every invocation. When restricted to a\r\n  subdirectory it additionally denies --copy-unsafe-links, passes the new\r\n  --confine-root so the server will not resolve a client-named filter merge\r\n  file outside that directory, and passes --drop-D when receiving, so an\r\n  upload can no longer create devices or special files there (\"skipping\r\n  non-regular file\"). A plain \"rsync -a\" otherwise still works.\r\n\r\n  --chmod=a+s now sets both the setuid and setgid bits, matching chmod(1);\r\n  it previously set setuid alone.\r\n\r\n  rsyncd changes that can change who gets in:\r\n   * \"proxy protocol = true\" without \"proxy protocol hosts\" now rejects\r\n     every connection and warns at startup, instead of trusting a\r\n     client-supplied PROXY header.\r\n   * \"hosts deny\" now fails closed when a configured hostname cannot be\r\n     resolved (with \"forward lookup\", the default), so a host previously\r\n     admitted by an unresolvable deny entry is now blocked.\r\n   * \"auth users\" values that start with a comma now split on commas alone,\r\n     as documented, so a deny or :ro rule naming a group whose name contains\r\n     a space now takes effect where it was silently ignored.\r\n   * \"hosts allow\" / \"hosts deny\" patterns now fold case inside a [...]\r\n     bracket expression as well, so a rule such as [A-Z]* matches hosts it\r\n     used to miss.\r\n   * A client-requested --compress-threads is capped at 8.\r\n\r\n  rsync-ssl now verifies the server certificate. The default openssl\r\n  backend additionally binds it to the requested hostname, so a certificate\r\n  valid for some other name is now rejected. The stunnel and gnutls\r\n  backends refuse to run unless RSYNC_SSL_CA_CERT is set, or\r\n  RSYNC_SSL_ALLOW_INSECURE_STUNNEL=1 / RSYNC_SSL_ALLOW_INSECURE_GNUTLS=1 is\r\n  set to opt out.\r\n\r\n -- Samuel Henrique \u003csamueloph@debian.org\u003e  Tue, 15 Sep 2026 18:46:30 -0700","submitter_user":"janus","user_is_author":true,"tags":["security"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/sqyhgt","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/sqyhgt/major_rsync_upgrade_debian_because_33"},{"short_id":"nwdxko","created_at":"2026-09-30T17:06:21.893-05:00","title":"EDG C/C++ Compiler Project","url":"https://github.com/edgcpp/compiler","score":28,"flags":0,"comment_count":10,"description":"\u003cp\u003eDuring the 2025 November C++ ISO meeting Edison Design Group \u003ca href=\"https://herbsutter.com/2025/11/10/trip-report-november-2025-iso-c-standards-meeting-kona-usa/\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003eannounced that they would be winding down and that they will open-source their front end\u003c/a\u003e. This is the repo containing said front end along with some other components like C and C++ back ends. There is also a \u003ca href=\"https://edgcpp.org/\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003ewebsite\u003c/a\u003e with more info about the transition and a \u003ca href=\"https://edgcpp.org/doc/\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003edocumentation website\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAt least as far as my knowledge extends this is one of the four still extant C++ front ends (Clang, GCC, and MSVC being the others). It's most notably used as the front end for Visual Studio's Intellisense, and Intel's compiler used to use it before they switched to Clang.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAnother interesting historical tidbit is that they were the only front end to implement C++98's \u003ccode\u003eexport template\u003c/code\u003e feature, and their experience was  \u003ca href=\"https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2003/n1426.pdf\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003eused to argue for the removal of the feature in C++11\u003c/a\u003e, though according to \u003ca href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/5416872/using-export-keyword-with-templates#comment6130296_5416981\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003ethis Stack Overflow comment\u003c/a\u003e that specific paper didn't pass voting. The feature was eventually removed in C++11, but I'm unable to find exactly when and/or which paper did so. There's a \u003ca href=\"https://herbsutter.com/2009/10/23/deprecating-export-considered-for-iso-c0x/\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003e2009 blog post by Herb Sutter\u003c/a\u003e indicating support for removal/deprecation with deprecation getting the most support, so I guess the paper/vote happened sometime after that.\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"During the 2025 November C++ ISO meeting Edison Design Group [announced that they would be winding down and that they will open-source their front end](https://herbsutter.com/2025/11/10/trip-report-november-2025-iso-c-standards-meeting-kona-usa/). This is the repo containing said front end along with some other components like C and C++ back ends. There is also a [website](https://edgcpp.org/) with more info about the transition and a [documentation website](https://edgcpp.org/doc/).\r\n\r\nAt least as far as my knowledge extends this is one of the four still extant C++ front ends (Clang, GCC, and MSVC being the others). It's most notably used as the front end for Visual Studio's Intellisense, and Intel's compiler used to use it before they switched to Clang.\r\n\r\nAnother interesting historical tidbit is that they were the only front end to implement C++98's `export template` feature, and their experience was  [used to argue for the removal of the feature in C++11](https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2003/n1426.pdf), though according to [this Stack Overflow comment](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/5416872/using-export-keyword-with-templates#comment6130296_5416981) that specific paper didn't pass voting. The feature was eventually removed in C++11, but I'm unable to find exactly when and/or which paper did so. There's a [2009 blog post by Herb Sutter](https://herbsutter.com/2009/10/23/deprecating-export-considered-for-iso-c0x/) indicating support for removal/deprecation with deprecation getting the most support, so I guess the paper/vote happened sometime after that.","submitter_user":"aw1621107","user_is_author":false,"tags":["c","c++","compilers"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/nwdxko","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/nwdxko/edg_c_c_compiler_project"},{"short_id":"bbi3w9","created_at":"2026-10-01T05:19:09.227-05:00","title":"EU KIDS Act","url":"https://commission.europa.eu/news-and-media/news/eu-kids-act-helping-children-navigate-safer-online-world-2026-09-17_en","score":20,"flags":1,"comment_count":4,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"hackguy","user_is_author":false,"tags":["culture","law","privacy"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/bbi3w9","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/bbi3w9/eu_kids_act"},{"short_id":"2r2sg8","created_at":"2026-09-30T14:57:54.701-05:00","title":"Finding Bugs","url":"https://matklad.github.io/2026/09/19/finding-bugs.html","score":31,"flags":